🐛 Describe the bug
The following graph shouldn't have any "aten" ops if traced under TorchRefsMode.push().
In [1]: import torch

In [2]: import torch._refs

In [3]: from torch.fx.experimental.proxy_tensor import make_fx

In [4]: from torch._prims.context import TorchRefsMode

In [5]: a = torch.randn(3, 3, device='cuda')

In [6]: with TorchRefsMode.push():
   ...:     gm = make_fx(lambda a: torch._refs.nn.functional.mish(a))(a)
   ...:

In [7]: gm.graph.print_tabular()
opcode         name    target      args              kwargs
-------------  ------  ----------  ----------------  --------
placeholder    a_1     a_1         ()                {}
call_function  exp     aten.exp    (a_1,)            {}
call_function  log1p   aten.log1p  (exp,)            {}
call_function  gt      aten.gt     (a_1, 20)         {}
call_function  where   aten.where  (gt, a_1, log1p)  {}
call_function  tanh    prims.tanh  (where,)          {}
call_function  mul     prims.mul   (a_1, tanh)       {}
output         output  output      (mul,)            {}
Using TorchRefsMode(strict=True) doesn't change anything.
torch._refs.nn.functional.mish calls torch._refs.nn.functional.softplus, tracing softplus directly works as expected.
Versions
Happens on master.
cc @ezyang @mruberry @ngimel

🐛 Describe the bug
Title says it all. I see the same behavior in python and C++.
Script:
import torch

torch.manual_seed(100)
torch.set_printoptions(precision=1, linewidth=150)

t0_cpu = (torch.rand((16,)) * 255).to(torch.uint8)
t0_mps = t0_cpu.to(torch.device('mps'))

t1_cpu = t0_cpu.to(torch.float32)
t1_mps = t0_mps.to(torch.float32)

print(f'CPU:\n{t0_cpu}\n{t1_cpu}\nMPS:\n{t0_mps}\n{t1_mps}')
Output:
CPU:
tensor([ 28, 208,  66, 123, 172, 192,  66,  10,  53,  30,  31, 187, 181, 200, 106, 229], dtype=torch.uint8)
tensor([ 28., 208.,  66., 123., 172., 192.,  66.,  10.,  53.,  30.,  31., 187., 181., 200., 106., 229.])
MPS:
tensor([ 28, 208,  66, 123, 172, 192,  66,  10,  53,  30,  31, 187, 181, 200, 106, 229], device='mps:0', dtype=torch.uint8)
tensor([ 28.0, -48.0,  66.0, 123.0, -84.0, -64.0,  66.0,  10.0,  53.0,  30.0,  31.0, -69.0, -75.0, -56.0, 106.0, -27.0], device='mps:0')

C++ example:
#include <torch/script.h>
#include <cstdio>

void print_tensor_uint8(torch::Tensor& t) {
    for (int i = 0; i < t.size(0); i++) { printf("%u\t", t.index({i}).item<uint8_t>()); }
    printf("\n");
}

void print_tensor_float(torch::Tensor& t) {
    for (int i = 0; i < t.size(0); i++) { printf("%0.2f\t", t.index({i}).item<float>()); }
    printf("\n");
}

int main() {
    torch::manual_seed(100);

    auto t0_cpu = (torch::rand({16}) * 255.f).toType(torch::kUInt8);
    auto t0_mps = t0_cpu.to(torch::kMPS);

    auto t1_cpu = t0_cpu.to(torch::kFloat32);
    auto t1_mps = t0_mps.to(torch::kFloat32);
    // this shows same behavior:
    // auto t1_cpu = t0_cpu.toType(torch::kFloat32);
    // auto t1_mps = t0_mps.toType(torch::kFloat32);

    auto diff_els = torch::abs(t1_cpu - t1_mps.to(torch::kCPU));

    auto total_diff = torch::sum(diff_els).item<float>();

    printf("CPU:\n");
    print_tensor_uint8(t0_cpu);
    print_tensor_float(t1_cpu);

    printf("MPS:\n");
    print_tensor_uint8(t0_mps);
    print_tensor_float(t1_mps);
}
